<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Malaysian Indian Congress</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1946">1946</a>–</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Malayan Indian Congress, MIC</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Malaysian Indian Congress, founded in 1946 as the Malayan Indian Congress, is a political party representing the Indian community of Malaysia, many of them descended from plantation labourers brought in under British rule. It joined the Alliance of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-malays-national-organisation">United Malays National Organisation</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaysian-chinese-association">Malayan Chinese Association</a> in 1954, sharing in the victory of 1955 and in independence in 1957, and remained a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barisan-nasional">Barisan Nasional</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/malaysian-indian-congress.jpg" alt="Malaysian Indian Congress" width="320" height="321" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Malaysian Indian Congress, redraw by Molecule Extraction · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Malaysian_Indian_Congress_Logo.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>John Thivy, who had met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> while studying law, founded the party in August 1946 to work for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-independence">Indian independence</a>, and took the word Congress from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-congress">Indian National Congress</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> became independent in 1947 the party turned to the independence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaya">Malaya</a>. Unlike most Indians in the country it opposed the Malayan Union, and it later joined the All-Malaya Council of Joint Action under Tun Tan Cheng Lock against the Federation of Malaya Agreement.</p><p>Its early leaders, Thivy, Baba Budh Singh Ji, K. Ramanathan and Kundan Lal Devaser, were of North Indian or Malayalee origin, while most Indians in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaya">Malaya</a> were Tamil plantation labourers whose wages, about 50 cents a day, rose and fell with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> prices. Defeated in the 1952 Kuala Lumpur Municipal Elections alongside the Independence of Malaya Party, the party joined the Alliance in 1954. In March 1955 the Tamil daily Tamil Murasu urged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamils">Tamils</a> to boycott it, and Devaser stepped down.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Tun V. T. Sambanthan, elected president in May 1955, recruited plantation workers by touring estates and by promoting the Tamil language, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a> in its South Indian form and Tamil culture, and under him the party became in effect a conservative Tamil party. It was the weakest of the three Alliance parties, with the smallest electorate, 7.4% in 1959. Sambanthan took part in the negotiations with the Reid Commission on the constitution, and in 1963, when Malaysia was formed, the party took the name Malaysian Indian Congress.</p><p>V. Manickavasagam, president from 1973 to 1978, saw the Alliance become the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barisan-nasional">Barisan Nasional</a> and set up the MIC Education Fund and the Nesa Multipurpose Cooperative. Samy Vellu led the party from 1979 to 2010; in 1984 it founded the Maju Institute of Education Development and in 2001 AIMST University. In the 2008 election Indian voters turned against the party and the Barisan Nasional, Vellu lost his seat, and support shifted to the Democratic Action Party and the People's Justice Party.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Malaysian Indian Congress” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Malaya became independent on 31 August 1957 under Tunku Abdul Rahman, leader of the Alliance of the United Malays National Organisation (UMNO), the Malayan Chinese Association and the <b>Malayan Indian Congress</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/malaysia">Malaysia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Alliance of UMNO, the Malayan Chinese Association and the <b>Malayan Indian Congress</b> won the 1955 election under Tunku Abdul Rahman, who negotiated independence on 31 August 1957.</blockquote><a href="/state/federation-of-malaya">Federation of Malaya</a></li></ul></section>
